---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import Code from '../../../components/docs/Code.astro';
import Table from '../../../components/docs/Table.astro';
import DeviceConstraints from '../../../components/docs/DeviceConstraints.astro';
import { page } from '../../../data/docs-nav';

const entry = page('/docs/destinations/influxdb/');

const lines = `health,type=heart_rate,source=Apple\\ Watch,device=Apple\\ Watch,unit=count/min value=62.5 1787433046500000000
health,type=sleep_analysis,source=Apple\\ Watch value=3.0,duration=1800.0 1787436000000000000
health_workouts,type=workout,activity=Running,source=Apple\\ Watch duration=1800.0,id="7b21c0de-0000-4000-8000-000000000001" 1787382000000000000
health_deletions,type=step_count,id=9c40c0de-0000-4000-8000-000000000002 deleted=true`;

const v2 = `POST http://influxdb.local:8086/api/v2/write?org=YOURORG&bucket=health&precision=ns
Authorization: Token YOUR_API_TOKEN
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8`;

const v1 = `POST http://influxdb.local:8086/write?db=health&precision=ns`;

const tags = [
  ['type', 'The metric name, e.g. heart_rate. workout for a workout.'],
  ['source', 'The app or device that recorded it. Omitted when unknown.'],
  ['device', 'The hardware name. Omitted when unknown.'],
  ['unit', 'HealthKit’s unit string. Omitted for types without one.'],
  ['activity', 'Workouts only — Running, Cycling, and so on.'],
];

const fields = [
  ['value', 'Float', 'Samples.'],
  ['duration', 'Float', 'Seconds. Present when the record covers an interval.'],
  ['id', 'String', 'Workouts and events, for tracing a point back to a record.'],
  ['deleted', 'Boolean', 'Tombstones.'],
];
---

<DocsLayout page={entry} section="Destinations">
  <div class="fact-strip">
    <div>
      <dt>Format</dt>
      <dd>InfluxDB line protocol</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Versions</dt>
      <dd>1.8, 2.x and 3.x</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Default measurement</dt>
      <dd><code>health</code></dd>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Hozz writes line protocol directly to InfluxDB's write endpoint. This exists because the usual
    self-hosted setup — Health data in InfluxDB, charted in Grafana — otherwise means deploying a
    container whose entire job is turning an exporter's JSON into the format InfluxDB already
    wanted. Writing it here removes that container from the diagram.
  </p>

  <h2>Setting it up</h2>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <h3>Put the whole write URL in the address field</h3>
      <p>
        Query string included. For 2.x and 3.x that is <code>/api/v2/write</code> with{' '}
        <code>org</code>, <code>bucket</code> and <code>precision</code>. For 1.8 it is{' '}
        <code>/write?db=yourdatabase</code>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Put the authorization value in the secret field</h3>
      <p>
        Hozz sends that secret as the <code>Authorization</code> header, so for 2.x and 3.x it has
        to be written as <code>Token</code> followed by a space and the token itself.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Set the measurement and precision under Details</h3>
      <p>
        The measurement defaults to <code>health</code>. The precision has to match the one in the
        address — see the warning below.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Send a test</h3>
      <p>
        InfluxDB writes one point you can query straight away. The probe is a single point in{' '}
        <code>&lt;measurement&gt;_events</code> tagged <code>type=hozz_connection_test</code>, and
        carries no Health data.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h3>InfluxDB 2.x and 3.x</h3>
  <Code caption="Address and secret" code={v2} />

  <h3>InfluxDB 1.8</h3>
  <Code caption="Address" code={v1} />

  <Callout kind="warn" title="Precision has to match, and InfluxDB will not tell you">
    <p>
      A point is timestamped in the precision configured on the destination. If that does not match
      the <code>precision</code> parameter on the write request, InfluxDB does not report a
      mismatch — it silently files every point in 1970 or in the far future.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nanoseconds is the default and the recommendation. Because InfluxDB stays quiet about it, Hozz
      reads the <code>precision</code> parameter back out of the address and warns in the
      destination editor when the two disagree.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>What gets written</h2>

  <Code caption="Line protocol" code={lines} />

  <h3>Measurements</h3>

  <Table caption="Measurements and what each holds" columns={['Measurement', 'Holds']} mono>
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">health</th>
      <td>Every sample with a numeric value.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">health_workouts</th>
      <td>Workouts.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">health_events</th>
      <td>Records with no numeric value at all, such as a correlation.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">health_deletions</th>
      <td>Tombstones.</td>
    </tr>
  </Table>

  <p>
    <code>health</code> is the configurable part; the four suffixes follow whatever you set it to.
  </p>

  <h3>Tags</h3>

  <Table caption="Tags written on each point" columns={['Tag', 'Value']} mono>
    {
      tags.map(([tag, value]) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">{tag}</th>
          <td>{value}</td>
        </tr>
      ))
    }
  </Table>

  <p>
    Every tag is deliberately low cardinality: a handful of sources, a handful of devices, a bounded
    set of types and units. <strong>Record identifiers are not tags</strong> — a UUID in a tag is the
    canonical way to ruin an InfluxDB instance — with one deliberate exception, described below.
  </p>

  <h3>Fields</h3>

  <Table caption="Fields written on each point" columns={['Field', 'Type', 'Where']} mono>
    {
      fields.map(([field, type, where]) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">{field}</th>
          <td>{type}</td>
          <td>{where}</td>
        </tr>
      ))
    }
  </Table>

  <h2>Two honest limitations</h2>

  <Callout kind="lossy" title="InfluxDB deduplicates, and that loses samples">
    <p>
      A point is identified by measurement, tag set and timestamp. Two samples of the same type,
      from the same source, at the same instant become one point. Line protocol has no way to
      express otherwise without putting a unique identifier in a tag, which would make the database
      unusable.
    </p>
    <p>
      The lossless formats do not have this property. If you need every sample kept separately, send{' '}
      <a href="/docs/delivery-schema/#ndjson">NDJSON</a> somewhere alongside.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <p>
    <strong>A deletion is recorded, not applied.</strong> Line protocol cannot retract a point, so
    tombstones go to <code>&lt;measurement&gt;_deletions</code> for you to reconcile yourself. That
    measurement is the one place Hozz puts a record identifier in a tag, and it is deliberate: a
    tombstone has no timestamp, so as a field every deletion in a batch would land on the same
    series at the same instant and overwrite the one before it. Not losing records is worth spending
    the cardinality on, and it is spent in a measurement nothing else queries.
  </p>

  <p>
    Hozz escapes exactly what the InfluxDB line protocol requires; non-finite values go to{' '}
    <code>&lt;measurement&gt;_events</code> rather than being dropped or invented.
  </p>

  <DeviceConstraints />
</DocsLayout>
